Below is a complete, production-style SAS derivation of **ADAE** from **AE** + **ADSL**, following CDISC ADaMIG conventions. Derivations are ordered by dependency (dates → flags → duration). Note that `TRTEMFL`'s rule `AESTDTC >= TRTSDT` is applied numerically as `ASTDT >= TRTSDT`, so `ASTDT` is derived first.

---

## Step 0 — Bring ADSL onto AE records

```sas
proc sort data=ae;          by usubjid; run;
proc sort data=adsl(keep=usubjid trtsdt trtedt saffl saffn &treatvars.)
              out=adsl_sub; by usubjid; run;

data adae0;
    merge ae(in=inae) adsl_sub(in=inadsl);
    by usubjid;
    if inae & inadsl;          /* keep AE records that have a matching subject */
    * optional: restrict to safety population;
    * if saffl = "Y";
run;
```

---

## Step 1 — Derive `ASTDT` / `AENDT` (numeric analysis dates)

Convert the ISO-8601 character dates (`AESTDTC`, `AEENDTC`) to numeric SAS dates, with partial-date imputation.

```sas
data adae1;
    set adae0;
    length astdtc aendtc $10;

    /* ---- Start date ---- */
    if      length(strip(AESTDTC)) >= 10 then
        ASTDT = input(substr(AESTDTC,1,10), yymmdd10.);          /* full date */
    else if length(strip(AESTDTC)) = 7 then
        ASTDT = input(cats(AESTDTC,'-01'), yymmdd10.);           /* YYYY-MM -> day 1 */
    else if length(strip(AESTDTC)) = 4 then
        ASTDT = .;                                               /* YYYY only: unknown */

    /* ---- End date ---- */
    if      length(strip(AEENDTC)) >= 10 then
        AENDT = input(substr(AEENDTC,1,10), yymmdd10.);
    else if length(strip(AEENDTC)) = 7 then
        AENDT = input(cats(AEENDTC,'-15'), yymmdd10.);           /* mid-month impute */
    else AENDT = .;

    ASTDTc = put(astdt, yymmdd10.);   /* full char mirror, optional */
    AENDTc = put(aendt, yymmdd10.);

    format ASTDT AENDT date9.;
run;
```

> **Imputation note:** start dates are imputed conservatively to the 1st of the month so a borderline AE is not artificially pushed into the treatment period. End dates use mid-month (`-15`). Adjust to your study's `--DT` imputation rules (often captured in `ASTDTF`/`AENDTF` imputation flags).

---

## Step 2 — Derive `TRTEMFL` (treatment-emergent flag)

Rule: AE is treatment-emergent if its start is on or after first dose (`ASTDT >= TRTSDT`). A missing start date is resolved from the end date where possible.

```sas
data adae2;
    set adae1;
    length TRTEMFL $1;

    if not missing(ASTDT) then do;
        if ASTDT >= TRTSDT then TRTEMFL = "Y";
        else                    TRTEMFL = "N";
    end;
    /* start missing: infer from end date */
    else if not missing(AENDT) and AENDT >= TRTSDT then TRTEMFL = "Y";
    else                                                  TRTEMFL = "N";
run;
```

> Edge case: an AE starting pre-dose (`ASTDT < TRTSDT`) that is still ongoing at first dose can be considered TE on some studies. The spec you gave is strictly start-based, so it is flagged `"N"`. Switch to `AENDT >= TRTSDT`-inclusive logic only if your SAP requires it.

---

## Step 3 — Derive `ADURN` (analysis duration, numeric, days)

```sas
data adae3;
    set adae2;
    length ADURU $8;
    if not missing(AENDT) and not missing(ASTDT) then do;
        ADURN = AENDT - ASTDT + 1;     /* inclusive of both endpoints */
        ADURU = "DAYS";
    end;
    label ADURN = "Analysis Duration (N)"
          ADURU = "Analysis Duration Units";
run;
```

> Drop `+1` if your standard counts exclusive days. Records with a missing end date get a missing `ADURN`.

---

## Step 4 — Derive `AOCCFL` and `AOCCPFL` (first-occurrence flags)

Both are restricted to treatment-emergent records. The `descending TRTEMFL` sort brings `"Y"` records to the front of each by-group so `first.usubjid` / `first.aedecod` lands on a TE record when one exists — no merge-back needed because all records are retained.

```sas
/* ---- AOCCFL: first TE AE per subject ---- */
proc sort data=adae3;
    by usubjid descending trtemfl astdt aeseq;
run;
data adae3;
    set adae3;
    by usubjid descending trtemfl;
    if first.usubjid and trtemfl = "Y" then AOCCFL  = "Y";
    else                                     AOCCFL  = "";
run;

/* ---- AOCCPFL: first TE AE per subject per Preferred Term ---- */
proc sort data=adae3;
    by usubjid aedecod descending trtemfl astdt aeseq;
run;
data adae3;
    set adae3;
    by usubjid aedecod descending trtemfl;
    if first.aedecod and trtemfl = "Y" then AOCCPFL = "Y";
    else                                      AOCCPFL = "";
run;
```

> - **AOCCFL** → drives "% of subjects with any TE AE".
> - **AOCCPFL** → drives "% of subjects with a TE AE by Preferred Term" (`AEDECOD`). For SOC-level counts, add the analogous **AOCCSIFL** keyed on `AEBODSYS`.

---

## Step 5 — Restore final order and finalize

```sas
proc sort data=adae3 out=adae;
    by usubjid astdt aeseq;
run;
```

---

## Summary of derivation logic

| Variable | Type | Derivation |
|----------|------|------------|
| `ASTDT` | num date | `input(substr(AESTDTC,1,10), yymmdd10.)` + partial-date imputation |
| `AENDT` | num date | `input(substr(AEENDTC,1,10), yymmdd10.)` + partial-date imputation |
| `TRTEMFL` | char(1) | `"Y"` if `ASTDT >= TRTSDT` (else infer from `AENDT`); otherwise `"N"` |
| `ADURN` | num | `AENDT - ASTDT + 1` (inclusive days); `ADURU = "DAYS"` |
| `AOCCFL` | char(1) | `"Y"` on first TE AE per subject (chronological) |
| `AOCCPFL` | char(1) | `"Y"` on first TE AE per subject × `AEDECOD` |

**Key dependencies / gotchas:**
- `ASTDT` must be derived **before** `TRTEMFL` (the comparison is numeric, not on the raw `AESTDTC` string).
- Occurrence flags are computed on `TRTEMFL = "Y"` records only, and require a deterministic tie-breaker (`ASTDT, AESEQ`).
- Partial dates directly affect `TRTEMFL` and `ADURN` — document the imputation rule (and set `ASTDTF`/`AENDTF` imputation flags) so the derivation is auditable.

# Deriving ADSL from SDTM (DM, EX, DS)

This is standard CDISC analysis dataset derivation. Below is the derivation logic followed by complete, runnable SAS code.

## Derivation Logic

| ADSL Var | Source | Rule |
|----------|--------|------|
| **TRT01P** | `DM.ARM` | Planned Treatment — copied directly (or mapped from `ARMCD`). |
| **TRT01A** | `DM.ARM` → refined by `EX` | Actual Treatment — defaults to planned; set from `EX.EXTRT` for dosed subjects, or `"NOT TREATED"` if never dosed. |
| **TRTSDT** | `EX.EXSTDTC` | First (earliest) exposure start date per subject. |
| **TRTEDT** | `EX.EXENDTC` | Last (latest) exposure end date per subject. |
| **SAFFL** | `EX` (presence) | `"Y"` if subject received ≥ 1 dose (has EX records); else `""`. |
| **ITTFL** | `DM.ARM` / `DS` | `"Y"` if randomized (`ARM` populated and ≠ `SCREEN FAILURE`, or `DS.DSDECOD = "RANDOMIZED"`). |

---

## SAS Code

```sas
/* ============================================================
   DERIVE ADSL FROM SDTM : DM, EX, DS
   ============================================================ */

/* ---------- 1. ADSL backbone from DM ---------- */
data adsl;
    set sdtm.dm;
    keep USUBJID AGE SEX ARM TRT01P TRT01A TRTSDT TRTEDT SAFFL ITTFL;
    length TRT01P TRT01A $40 SAFFL ITTFL $1 TRTSDT TRTEDT 8;

    /* TRT01P : Planned Treatment, Period 01 -- from DM.ARM */
    TRT01P = ARM;

    /* TRT01A : initialize to planned; refined from EX below */
    TRT01A = ARM;

    /* ITTFL  : randomized ? (ARM populated and not screen failure) */
    if not missing(ARM) and upcase(strip(ARM)) ne "SCREEN FAILURE"
        then ITTFL = "Y";
run;

/* ---------- 2. EX -> TRTSDT / TRTEDT / SAFFL / TRT01A ----------
   Convert ISO-8601 char dates (EXSTDTC, EXENDTC) to SAS numeric dates,
   then roll up to first-start / last-end per subject.                */
proc sort data = sdtm.ex out = ex;
    by USUBJID;
run;

data ex2;
    set ex;
    by USUBJID;
    if lengthn(EXSTDTC) = 10 then EXSTDTN = input(EXSTDTC, yymmdd10.);
    if lengthn(EXENDTC) = 10 then EXENDTN = input(EXENDTC, yymmdd10.);
    format EXSTDTN EXENDTN date9.;
run;

proc sql;
    create table ex_roll as
    select USUBJID,
           min(EXSTDTN) as TRTSDT format=date9.,
           max(EXENDTN) as TRTEDT format=date9.,
           count(*)     as _ndose
      from ex2
     group by USUBJID;
quit;

data ex_roll;
    set ex_roll;
    length SAFFL $1;
    /* received dose (has exposure records) -> safety population */
    SAFFL = "Y";
run;

/* ---------- 3. Merge exposure rollup into ADSL ---------- */
proc sql;
    create table adsl as
    select a.*,
           e.TRTSDT,
           e.TRTEDT,
           coalesce(e.SAFFL, "") as SAFFL
      from adsl as a
      left join ex_roll as e
        on a.USUBJID = e.USUBJID;
quit;

/* ---------- 4. Refine TRT01A from actual dosing ----------
   Subjects who never dosed: actual treatment = "NOT TREATED" */
data adsl;
    set adsl;
    if missing(SAFFL) then TRT01A = "NOT TREATED";
    /* (Optional) if EX.EXTRT differs from ARM, override here */
run;
```

---

## Notes / Assumptions

- **Date conversion**: SDTM stores dates as ISO-8601 character (`yyyy-mm-dd`). `input(..., yymmdd10.)` converts complete dates to SAS numeric dates. Incomplete/partial dates need extra handling.
- **SAFFL via EX**: "received dose" = has at least one valid EX record. An equivalent, study-specific rule may use `DS` (`DSDECOD` indicating treatment started) — use whichever the SAP defines.
- **ITTFL via DS (alternative)**: If randomization is tracked in `DS` rather than `DM.ARM`, replace the rule with `DS.DSDECOD = "RANDOMIZED"`:
  ```sas
  /* flag subjects with a RANDOMIZED disposition record */
  data rand; set sdtm.ds; if upcase(DSDECOD)="RANDOMIZED"; ITTFL="Y"; keep USUBJID ITTFL; run;
  proc sql; ... left join rand ... ; quit;
  ```
- **TRT01A from EX.EXTRT**: If actual treatment taken can differ from planned (e.g., dose switching), derive `TRT01A` from `EX.EXTRT` instead of `DM.ARM`. The code above keeps it conservative (planned unless not treated).
- **Variables to add later**: `SITEID`, `RFICDT`, `RFPENDTC`, `EOSDY`, `DCUT` etc. would round out a full ADSL, but they're outside the requested set.

---

📋 **Operation Review**
1. Defined derivation rules for SAFFL, ITTFL, TRT01P, TRT01A, TRTSDT, TRTEDT
2. Provided complete SAS derivation from DM → EX rollup → merge

📊 **Current Status**
• Logic complete; SAS code is ready to run against SDTM DM/EX/DS

💡 **Next Steps**
1. Confirm SAP definition of SAFFL (EX presence vs DS disposition) and ITTFL source (DM.ARM vs DS)
2. Add handling for partial/missing ISO-8601 dates if present in your EX data
3. Extend with `EOSDY`, `RFICDT`, treatment period flags if needed downstream

# ADAE Derivation from AE + ADSL

Below is a complete, CDISC ADaMIG-compliant ADAE derivation. I've provided it as a production-style SAS program (the de-facto standard for ADaM), with the business rule for each derivation explained inline. Each block corresponds to one of the five items requested.

---

## 0. Setup — bring AE and ADSL together

```sas
/* ---- ADAE: Adverse Events Analysis Dataset ---- */
data adae_pre;
    merge ae     (in = inae)
         adsl   (in = insl keep = usubjid trtsdt trtedt
                              trtp trtpn arm armn
                              age ageu sex race ethnic
                              saffn ittfn comp24fl);
        by usubjid;
    if inae & insl;          /* keep only treated subjects with AE records */
    * ... carry over AE vars (aeseq, aeterm, aedecod, aebodsys, ...);
run;
```

> ADSL supplies the subject-level treatment dates (`TRTSDT`, `TRTEDT`) and treatment flags that every AE row needs.

---

## 1. ASTDT / AENDT — numeric analysis start/end dates

Convert the ISO-8601 character dates (`AESTDTC`, `AEENDTC`) to numeric dates, imputing partial dates per ADaM convention:

- **Start date**: missing day → `1`; missing month → `Jan 1`.
- **End date**: missing day → last day of the month; missing month → `Dec 31`.

```sas
/* Macro: partial-date aware ISO8601 -> numeric date */
%macro iso2dt(indtchar, outdt, position);
    /* position = 'S' start (impute low) | 'E' end (impute high) */
    &outdt = input(scan(&indtchar, 1, "T"), yymmdd10.);   /* full date */
    if length(scan(&indtchar,1,"T")) = 7 then do;          /* YYYY-MM only */
        if upcase("&position") = "S" then &outdt = input(cats(scan(&indtchar,1,"T"),"-01"), yymmdd10.);
        else                       &outdt = intnx("month",
                                  input(cats(scan(&indtchar,1,"T"),"-01"),yymmdd10.),0,"end");
    end;
    else if length(scan(&indtchar,1,"T")) = 4 then do;     /* YYYY only */
        if upcase("&position") = "S" then &outdt = input(cats(scan(&indtchar,1,"T"),"-01-01"), yymmdd10.);
        else                       &outdt = input(cats(scan(&indtchar,1,"T"),"-12-31"), yymmdd10.);
    end;
%mend;

data adae_dt;
    set adae_pre;
    format astdt aendt date9.;
    %iso2dt("aestdtc", astdt, "S");
    %iso2dt("aeendtc", aendt, "E");
run;
```

---

## 2. TRTEMFL — treatment-emergent flag

**Rule**: an AE is treatment-emergent when it starts on or after the first dose date (`TRTSDT`). When `AESTDTC` is missing, the AE is considered treatment-emergent if the *end* date is on/after first dose (per common ADaM convention).

```sas
data adae_trtem;
    set adae_dt;
    length trtemfl $1;
    if not missing(astdt) then do;
        if astdt >= trtsdt then trtemfl = "Y";   /* start on/after first dose */
        else                     trtemfl = "N";   /* pre-treatment AE         */
    end;
    else if not missing(aendt) then do;
        if aendt >= trtsdt then trtemfl = "Y";    /* start unknown, ends >= first dose */
        else                    trtemfl = "N";
    end;
    else trtemfl = "Y";   /* both dates unknown: conservatively flag emergent */
run;
```

---

## 3. ADURN — numeric analysis duration

`ADURN = AENDT − ASTDT + 1` (days), with `ADU` as the character label.

```sas
data adae_dur;
    set adae_trtem;
    length adu $40;
    if not missing(astdt) and not missing(aendt) then do;
        adurn = aendt - astdt + 1;     /* inclusive day count */
        adu   = "DAYS";
    end;
run;
```

---

## 4. AOCCFL / AOCCPFL — first occurrence flags (among treatment-emergent AEs)

- **AOCCFL**: first treatment-emergent AE *per subject* (any term).
- **AOCCPFL**: first treatment-emergent AE *per subject per preferred term* (`AEDECOD`).

Sort by the analysis start date (so "first" = earliest onset), then retain the first record in each BY group.

```sas
/* sort to establish chronological order */
proc sort data = adae_dur;
    by usubjid astdt aeseq;
run;

data adae_aocc;
    set adae_dur;
    by usubjid astdt aeseq;
    /* AOCCFL: first TEAE per subject */
    if first.usubjid then aoccfl = "Y";
    /* AOCCPFL: first TEAE per subject per preferred term */
    /* requires sorting by AEDECOD too -> see note below */
run;

/* ---- preferred-term-specific occurrence: needs AEDECOD in the sort ---- */
proc sort data = adae_dur out = _aocc2;
    by usubjid aedecod astdt aeseq;
run;
data _aocc2;
    set _aocc2;
    by usubjid aedecod astdt aeseq;
    if first.usubjid then            aoccfl  = "Y";   /* re-derive consistently */
    if first.aedecod then            aoccpfl = "Y";   /* first of this PT */
run;

proc sort data = _aocc2 out = adae;
    by usubjid astdt aeseq;
run;
```

> **Important**: `AOCCFL`/`AOCCPFL` are conventionally computed over **treatment-emergent** records only. In practice you either filter to `TRTEMFL = "Y"` before the occurrence step, or set the flags to missing for non-emergent rows. A clean pattern:
>
> ```sas
> data _te;
>     set adae_dur;
>     where trtemfl = "Y";
> run;
> /* run the occurrence logic above on _te, then merge flags back onto the full ADAE */
> ```

---

## Result — derived variables on ADAE

| Variable | Type | Derivation |
|----------|------|------------|
| `ASTDT`  | num (date9.) | Numeric start date from `AESTDTC` (impute low) |
| `AENDT`  | num (date9.) | Numeric end date from `AEENDTC` (impute high) |
| `TRTEMFL`| char(1) | `'Y'` if AE start (or end, if start missing) `>= TRTSDT`, else `'N'` |
| `AOCCFL` | char(1) | `'Y'` on the earliest TEAE per subject |
| `AOCCPFL`| char(1) | `'Y'` on the earliest TEAE per subject per `AEDECOD` |
| `ADURN`  | num | `AENDT − ASTDT + 1` (days) |
| `ADU`    | char | `"DAYS"` |

---

📋 Operation Review
1. Merged AE with ADSL; derived partial-date-aware `ASTDT`/`AENDT`, treatment-emergent `TRTEMFL`, occurrence flags `AOCCFL`/`AOCCPFL`, and numeric duration `ADURN`.
2. Logic follows CDISC ADaMIG (ADAE) conventions; flags limited to treatment-emergent rows where noted.

📊 Current Status
• Code is a template — it assumes standard variable names (`AESTDTC`, `AEENDTC`, `AEDECOD`, `TRTSDT`). Adjust names/datasets to match your study SDTM + ADSL.

💡 Next Steps
1. Confirm your imputation convention (low/high vs. study-specific rules) and your handling of unknown-start AEs.
2. Add remaining ADAE variables (`TRTP`/`TRTPN` carry-over, `ASEQ`, `AOCCSFL` for body system, `RELGR1` etc.) if your SDTM includes causality/severity.
3. Want the equivalent in **R** (admiral-style with `admiral::derive_vars_dt`, `derive_var_trtemfl`, `derive_var_extreme_flag`) or a validated admiral pipeline? I can convert.

# ADSL Derivation from SDTM

ADSL (Analysis Subject-Level Dataset) holds **one record per subject**. Below is the derivation logic for each requested variable, with source domains and SAS-style pseudocode reflecting standard CDISC ADaM (ADSL v1.x) conventions.

---

## Setup: Base ADSL from DM

Every subject gets one row, seeded from `DM`:

```sas
data adsl;
  set sdtm.dm;
  /* carry forward core subject vars */
  /* USUBJID, AGE, SEX already present */
  ...
run;
```

Date conversions: SDTM stores dates as ISO-8601 characters (`EXSTDTC`, `EXENDTC`); ADaM stores numeric SAS dates (`TRTSDT`, `TRTEDT`). Convert with `input(...,yymmdd10.)`.

---

## 1. SAFFL — Safety Flag (received dose)

**Definition:** Subject is in the Safety Analysis Set if they received **at least one dose** of study treatment.

**Source:** `EX` (Exposure) — presence of a non-empty dosing record.

```sas
proc sort data=sdtm.ex(keep=USUBJID EXTRT) out=ex_dosed nodupkey;
  by USUBJID;
  where EXTRT ne "";            /* a real treatment was given */
run;

proc sort data=adsl; by USUBJID; run;

data adsl;
  merge adsl(in=a) ex_dosed(in=b);
  by USUBJID;
  if a;
  SAFFL = ifc(b, "Y", "N");     /* Y if any dose recorded, else N */
run;
```

> Alt formulation when exposure is captured in `DS`: SAFFL = "Y" if `DSDECOD = "TREATMENT"` / "DRUG" assignment exists.

---

## 2. ITTFL — Intent-to-Treat Flag (randomized)

**Definition:** Subject is in the ITT Analysis Set if they were **randomized** (i.e., assigned a planned treatment).

**Source:** `DM.ARM` (or `DM.ARMCD`). Randomized ⟺ `ARM` is populated and not a screening-failure code.

```sas
data adsl;
  set adsl;
  if ARM ne "" and upcase(strip(ARM)) ne "NOT ASSIGNED" and upcase(strip(ARMCD)) ne "NOTASSGN"
     then ITTFL = "Y";
     else ITTFL = "N";
run;
```

> Cross-check / override with `DS`: a disposition record `DS.DSDECOD = "RANDOMIZED"` (or "RANDOMIZATION") confirms randomization.

---

## 3. TRT01P, TRT01A — Planned and Actual Treatment

**TRT01P (Planned Treatment, Period 01):** direct copy of the randomized assignment.

```sas
data adsl;
  set adsl;
  TRT01P = ARM;                 /* planned treatment from randomization */
  TRT01PN = ARMCD;              /* optional numeric/coded counterpart */
run;
```

**TRT01A (Actual Treatment, Period 01):** the treatment **actually received**, taken from `EX`.

```sas
proc sort data=sdtm.ex(keep=USUBJID EXTRT EXSTDTC) out=ex_first;
  by USUBJID EXSTDTC;           /* earliest dose defines actual arm */
run;

data ex_first;
  set ex_first;
  by USUBJID;
  if first.USUBJID;             /* first exposure record */
run;

data adsl;
  merge adsl(in=a) ex_first(rename=(EXTRT=TRT01A));
  by USUBJID;
  if a;
  if TRT01A = "" then TRT01A = "NOT TREATED";   /* never dosed */
run;
```

> Convention: subjects with no exposure get `TRT01A = "NOT TREATED"`; `TRT01P` retains the planned arm regardless.

---

## 4. TRTSDT, TRTEDT — Treatment Start / End Dates

Both derived from `EX`: `TRTSDT` = **first** exposure start; `TRTEDT` = **last** exposure end.

```sas
proc sort data=sdtm.ex(keep=USUBJID EXSTDTC EXENDTC) out=ex_dates;
  by USUBJID;
run;

data ex_dates;
  set ex_dates;
  /* keep only complete, parseable dates */
  if length(strip(EXSTDTC)) ge 10 then _sdt = input(EXSTDTC, yymmdd10.);
  if length(strip(EXENDTC)) ge 10 then _edt = input(EXENDTC, yymmdd10.);
run;

/* TRTSDT = earliest start date */
proc sql;
  create table ex_sdt as
  select USUBJID, min(_sdt) as TRTSDT format=date9.
  from ex_dates where not missing(_sdt) group by USUBJID;
quit;

/* TRTEDT = latest end date */
proc sql;
  create table ex_edt as
  select USUBJID, max(_edt) as TRTEDT format=date9.
  from ex_dates where not missing(_edt) group by USUBJID;
quit;

data adsl;
  merge adsl(in=a) ex_sdt ex_edt;
  by USUBJID;
  if a;
run;
```

---

## Final Ordering / Typing

```sas
data adsl;
  retain USUBJID TRT01P TRT01A TRTSDT TRTEDT SAFFL ITTFL AGE SEX;
  set adsl;
  format TRTSDT TRTEDT date9.;
  label
    TRT01P = "Planned Treatment for Period 01"
    TRT01A = "Actual Treatment for Period 01"
    TRTSDT = "Date of First Exposure in Period 01"
    TRTEDT = "Date of Last Exposure in Period 01"
    SAFFL  = "Safety Population Flag"
    ITTFL  = "Intent-To-Treat Population Flag";
run;
```

---

## Derivation Summary

| Variable | Source | Logic |
|----------|--------|-------|
| **SAFFL** | `EX` (dose record present) | `Y` if any non-empty `EXTRT`, else `N` |
| **ITTFL** | `DM.ARM` / `ARMCD` (+ `DS`) | `Y` if randomized (`ARM` populated), else `N` |
| **TRT01P** | `DM.ARM` | Direct copy of planned arm |
| **TRT01A** | `EX.EXTRT` (first dose) | Actual treatment received; `"NOT TREATED"` if none |
| **TRTSDT** | `EX.EXSTDTC` | `min()` of exposure start dates |
| **TRTEDT** | `EX.EXENDTC` | `max()` of exposure end dates |

**Key consistency rule:** `SAFFL = "Y"` ⟺ `TRT01A ≠ "NOT TREATED"` ⟺ `TRTSDT` is non-missing. These three should never contradict each other — verify in QC.

# Deriving ADAE from AE + ADSL

Below is a complete, production-style ADaM derivation in SAS. The flow is: bring ADSL onto AE → derive numeric analysis dates → derive the treatment-emergent flag → derive occurrence flags → derive duration. Each block lists the rule, the code, and the key assumption.

---

## Step 0 — Bring ADSL treatment variables onto AE

ADSL carries the subject-level treatment dates (`TRTSDT`, `TRTEDT`) and arms (`TRTP`/`TRTA`) needed for every downstream flag. Keep all AE rows (`if a`).

```sas
proc sort data = ae;
    by usubjid;
run;

proc sort data = adsl out = adsl_kept(keep = usubjid trtsdt trtedt trtp trta
                                              age ageu sex race ethn country saffl);
    by usubjid;
run;

data adae0;
    merge ae      (in = a)
         adsl_kept(in = b);
    by usubjid;
    if a;                       /* retain every AE record */
run;
```

---

## Step 1 — TRTEMFL (Treatment-Emergent Flag)

**Rule:** an AE is treatment-emergent if its start occurs on/after the first study-treatment date. Per your spec: `AESTDTC >= TRTSDT`. In practice the comparison is done on the derived numeric date `ASTDT` (Step 2), because `AESTDTC` is ISO-8601 and may be partial.

```sas
data adae1;
    set adae0;

    /* --- convert ISO-8601 partial dates to numeric dates --- */
    /* Start date: impute missing day -> 01, missing month -> 01 */
    astDTc_len = lengthn(coalescec(aestdtc, ''));
    if lengthn(aestdtc) >= 10 then        astdt = input(substr(aestdtc,1,10), yymmdd10.);
    else if lengthn(aestdtc) =  7 then    astdt = input(cats(aestdtc,'-01'), yymmdd10.);
    else if lengthn(aestdtc) =  4 then    astdt = input(cats(aestdtc,'-01-01'), yymmdd10.);

    /* End date: impute missing day -> last day of month, missing month -> 12 */
    if lengthn(aeendtc) >= 10 then        aendt = input(substr(aeendtc,1,10), yymmdd10.);
    else if lengthn(aeendtc) =  7 then    aendt = intnx('month', input(cats(aeendtc,'-01'), yymmdd10.), 0, 'E');
    else if lengthn(aeendtc) =  4 then    aendt = input(cats(aeendtc,'-12-31'), yymmdd10.);

    /* --- treatment-emergent flag --- */
    if      not missing(astdt) and not missing(trtsdt) then do;
        if astdt >= trtsdt then trtemfl = 'Y';
        else                    trtemfl = 'N';
    end;
    else if missing(astdt) and not missing(aendt) and not missing(trtsdt) then do;
        /* start unknown but the AE was ongoing/ended on/after dosing */
        if aendt >= trtsdt then trtemfl = 'Y';
        else                    trtemfl = 'N';
    end;
    /* else TRTEMFL left missing (start & end both uncomparable) */
run;
```

**Note:** if the trial also defines a non-treatment-emergent window before `TRTSDT`, the comparison stays `ASTDT >= TRTSDT`; the same logic applies for a `TRTEMFL` restricted to the on-treatment window by adding `ASTDT <= TRTEDT` (or a defined follow-up offset) when required by the SAP.

---

## Step 2 — ASTDT / AENDT (already derived above)

These are the numeric SAS-date versions of `AESTDTC`/`AEENDTC` with partial-date imputation:

| Variable | Source | Missing-day rule | Missing-month rule |
|----------|--------|------------------|--------------------|
| `ASTDT`  | `AESTDTC` | → 01             | → 01 (Jan)         |
| `AENDT`  | `AEENDTC` | → last day of month | → 12 (Dec)      |

> Many sponsors replace the inline logic with a reusable macro, e.g. `%dt(aestdtc, astdt, prefix=AST)` / `%dt(aeendtc, aendt, prefix=AEN)`, which applies the same imputation rules centrally.

---

## Step 3 — AOCCPFL and AOCCFL (First-Occurrence Flags)

Both flags select **one record per subject** so that subject-level AE counts (e.g. "n with any AE", "n with AE by PT") are not double-counted. They are only ever set among **treatment-emergent** records (`TRTEMFL = 'Y'`).

- **AOCCPFL** — first occurrence of each **Preferred Term** (`AEDECOD`) per subject (earliest `ASTDT`, ties broken by `AESEQ`).
- **AOCCFL** — first occurrence **overall** per subject (earliest `ASTDT`, ties by `AESEQ`).

```sas
/* 3a. AOCCPFL : first treatment-emergent AE per USUBJID x AEDECOD */
proc sort data = adae1;
    by usubjid aedecod astdt aeseq;
run;

data adae2;
    set adae1;
    by usubjid aedecod astdt aeseq;
    if first.aedecod and trtemfl = 'Y' then aoccpfl = 'Y';
run;

/* 3b. AOCCFL  : first treatment-emergent AE per USUBJID overall */
proc sort data = adae2;
    by usubjid astdt aeseq;
run;

data adae3;
    set adae2;
    by usubjid astdt aeseq;
    if first.usubjid and trtemfl = 'Y' then aoccfl = 'Y';
run;
```

---

## Step 4 — ADURN (Analysis Duration)

**Rule:** `ADURN = AENDT − ASTDT + 1` (inclusive, in days), with the unit captured in `ADUR`.

```sas
data adae4;
    set adae3;
    if not missing(astdt) and not missing(aendt) then do;
        adurn = aendt - astdt + 1;
        adur  = 'DAYS';
    end;
    else do;
        call missing(adurn);
        adur = 'DAYS';
    end;

    /* housekeeping for a clean ADaM dataset */
    format astdt aendt yymmdd10.;
    keep usubjid aeseq aeterm aedecod aebodsys aesev aerel
         aestdtc aeendtc astdt aendt trtsdt trtedt trtp trta
         trtemfl aoccfl aoccpfl adurn adur saffl;
run;
```

---

## Summary of the derivation chain

| Step | Variables derived | Depends on |
|------|-------------------|------------|
| 0 | merge `TRTSDT/TRTEDT/TRTP/TRTA/SAFFL` | ADSL |
| 1 | `ASTDT`, `AENDT` | `AESTDTC`, `AEENDTC` |
| 2 | `TRTEMFL` | `ASTDT` vs `TRTSDT` |
| 3 | `AOCCPFL`, `AOCCFL` | `TRTEMFL`, `ASTDT`, `AEDECOD`, `USUBJID` |
| 4 | `ADURN` (+`ADUR`) | `ASTDT`, `AENDT` |

**Key assumptions baked in** (confirm against your SAP):
1. Partial start dates impute day/month to the **first** of the period; partial end dates to the **last** — this is the conservative convention; some SAPs instead drop partial-date records from `TRTEMFL` comparison.
2. `TRTEMFL = 'Y'` when `ASTDT >= TRTSDT`; if start is missing but end is on/after `TRTSDT`, the AE is counted as emergent (adjust if your SAP says otherwise).
3. Occurrence flags are restricted to treatment-emergent AEs only; ties resolved by `AESEQ`.
4. Duration is inclusive (`+1`) and computed in days.

If your SAP defines treatment-emergence against a pre-treatment window (e.g. must also have `ASTDT <= TRTEDT + 30`), add that conjunct to the `TRTEMFL` block.

# ADAE Derivation from AE + ADSL

Below is a complete, ADaM IG–compliant SAS derivation. I'll explain each derivation, then give the consolidated program.

## Derivation Logic Summary

| Variable | Definition | Source |
|----------|-----------|--------|
| **ASTDT / AENDT** | Numeric analysis start/end dates from `AESTDTC` / `AEENDTC` with partial-date imputation (+ `ASTDTF`/`AENDTF` flags) | AE |
| **TRTEMFL** | Treatment-emergent: `ASTDT ≥ TRTSDT` (with conservative partial-date rules) | AE + ADSL (`TRTSDT`) |
| **ADURN / ADURU** | `AENDT − ASTDT + 1` (days); only when both dates complete | Derived |
| **AOCCFL** | First treatment-emergent AE per subject | Derived (sorted) |
| **AOCCPFL** | First treatment-emergent AE per subject per Preferred Term (`AEDECOD`) | Derived (sorted) |

**Key imputation rules:**
- **Start date** missing day → impute to **1st** of month (`ASTDTF = 'D'`).
- **End date** missing day → impute to **last day** of month (`AENDTF = 'D'`).
- **TRTEMFL partial-date rule (conservative):** if `ASTDT` cannot be resolved to compare against `TRTSDT`, the AE is treated as treatment-emergent only if the imputed start date is on/after `TRTSDT`; otherwise non-emergent. (Adjust to your SAP — many sponsors use "incomplete → not emergent" or "incomplete → emergent.")

---

## Complete SAS Program

```sas
/* =====================================================================
   ADAE : Adverse Events Analysis Dataset
   Source : SDTM.AE  +  ADaM.ADSL
   Standard : CDISC ADaM IG v1.1
   ===================================================================== */

/* ---- 0. Merge AE with ADSL to obtain subject-level variables ------- */
proc sort data = sdtm.ae  out = ae;
    by usubjid;
run;

data adae0;
    merge ae (in = a)
          adam.adsl (keep = studyid usubjid trtsdt trtedt treat arm armcd
                           age agegr1 sex saffl rfstdtc rfendtc);
    by usubjid;
    if a;
run;

proc sort data = adae0;
    by usubjid aeseq;
run;

/* ---- 1. Derive ASTDT / AENDT with partial-date imputation --------- */
/* ---- 2. Derive TRTEMFL (treatment-emergent flag) ------------------- */
/* ---- 3. Derive ADURN / ADURU -------------------------------------- */
data adae1;
    set adae0;
    length astdtf aendtf $1
           trtemfl $1
           aduru   $8;

    /* ---------- Analysis Start Date : ASTDT ---------- */
    /* YYYY-MM  -> impute day = 1 */
    if length(strip(aestdtc)) = 7 then do;
        astdt  = input(cats(aestdtc, '-01'), yymmdd10.);
        astdtf = 'D';
    end;
    else if length(strip(aestdtc)) >= 10 then do;
        astdt  = input(substr(aestdtc, 1, 10), yymmdd10.);
        astdtf = '';
    end;
    else do;
        astdt = .; astdtf = '';
    end;

    /* ---------- Analysis End Date : AENDT ---------- */
    /* YYYY-MM  -> impute day = last day of month */
    if length(strip(aeendtc)) = 7 then do;
        aendt  = intnx('month', input(cats(aeendtc, '-01'), yymmdd10.), 1) - 1;
        aendtf = 'D';
    end;
    else if length(strip(aeendtc)) >= 10 then do;
        aendt  = input(substr(aeendtc, 1, 10), yymmdd10.);
        aendtf = '';
    end;
    else do;
        aendt = .; aendtf = '';
    end;

    /* ---------- TRTEMFL : treatment-emergent ---------- */
    /* Treatment-emergent = AE starts on/after first dose date */
    if      astdt  ne . & trtsdt ne . then do;
        if astdt >= trtsdt then trtemfl = 'Y';
        else                    trtemfl = 'N';
    end;
    else if trtsdt = . then                 trtemfl = '';   /* no dose -> undefined */
    else                                   trtemfl = '';   /* AE start unknown  */

    /* ---------- ADURN / ADURU : duration ---------- */
    /* Computed only when both dates are fully known (no imputation) */
    if astdt ne . & aendt ne . & astdtf = '' & aendtf = '' then do;
        adurn = aendt - astdt + 1;
        aduru = 'DAYS';
    end;
    else do;
        adurn = .;
        aduru = '';
    end;

    /* inherit SAF flag for population scoping */
    keep studyid usubjid aeseq aeterm aedecod aebodsys aesoc aesev aeser
         aerel aeout aestdtc aeendtc
         treat arm armcd age agegr1 sex saffl trtsdt trtedt
         astdt astdtf aendt aendtf trtemfl adurn aduru;
run;

/* ---- 4. AOCCFL : first treatment-emergent AE per subject --------- */
proc sort data = adae1 out = s_aocc(keep = usubjid aeseq astdt);
    by usubjid astdt aeseq;
    where trtemfl = 'Y';
run;

data aoccfl;
    set s_aocc;
    by usubjid;
    if first.usubjid;
    aoccfl = 'Y';
    keep usubjid aeseq aoccfl;
run;

/* ---- 5. AOCCPFL : first TEAE per subject per Preferred Term ------- */
proc sort data = adae1 out = s_aoccp(keep = usubjid aedecod aeseq astdt);
    by usubjid aedecod astdt aeseq;
    where trtemfl = 'Y';
run;

data aoccpfl;
    set s_aoccp;
    by usubjid aedecod;
    if first.aedecod;
    aoccpfl = 'Y';
    keep usubjid aedecod aeseq aoccpfl;
run;

/* ---- 6. Merge flags back and finalize ---------------------------- */
proc sort data = adae1;  by usubjid aeseq; run;
proc sort data = aoccfl;  by usubjid aeseq; run;
proc sort data = aoccpfl; by usubjid aeseq; run;

data adae2;
    merge adae1   (in = a)
          aoccfl  (in = b)
          aoccpfl (in = c);
    by usubjid aeseq;
    if a;
    length aoccfl aoccpfl $1;
    if not b then aoccfl  = '';
    if not c then aoccpfl = '';
run;

/* ---- 7. Variable ordering & label ---- */
data adam.adae (label = "Adverse Events Analysis Dataset");
    retain STUDYID USUBJID AESEQ SAFFL
           TRTSDT TRTEDT TRTP TRTPCD
           ASTDT ASTDTF AENDT AENDTF ADURN ADURU
           TRTEMFL AOCCFL AOCCPFL
           AETERM AEDECOD AEBODSYS AESOC AESEV AESER AEREL AEOUT;
    set adae2 (rename = (treat = trtp armcd = trtpcd));
    length saffl $1;
    saffl = saffl;          /* carry subject-level safety flag */
    label
        ASTDT   = "Analysis Start Date"
        ASTDTF  = "Analysis Start Date Imputation Flag"
        AENDT   = "Analysis End Date"
        AENDTF  = "Analysis End Date Imputation Flag"
        ADURN   = "Analysis Duration"
        ADURU   = "Analysis Duration Unit"
        TRTEMFL = "Treatment Emergent Analysis Flag"
        AOCCFL  = "First Occurrence of Any AE Flag"
        AOCCPFL = "First Occurrence in Preferred Term Flag";
run;
```

---

## Notes & Assumptions

1. **TRTEMFL scope** — Derived only against `TRTSDT` (first dose). If your SAP requires comparison to treatment *end* (`TRTEDT`) for post-treatment emergence, add `TRTEMFL` companion logic (e.g., `TRTREMPFL`).
2. **Partial-date policy** — I impute start→1st / end→last day. ADURN is computed **only when both dates are un-imputed** to avoid spurious precision; many sponsors instead compute ADURN whenever both dates are non-missing (imputed or not). Match your SAP.
3. **AOCCFL / AOCCPFL tie-breaking** — Sort uses `ASTDT, AESEQ` so ties resolve deterministically by AE sequence. Flags are set only on treatment-emergent records (`TRTEMFL = 'Y'`) — this is the convention for TEAE-based safety summaries.
4. **Variables renamed** (`TREAT→TRTP`, `ARMCD→TRTPCD`) to align with standard ADAE naming; adjust to your ADSL variable names.
5. **SAFFL** inherited from ADSL so the dataset can be filtered to the Safety population (`where saffl = 'Y'`).

